>If the average chance to find a spellbook in an oracle statue is 16.4%, then 
>the chance of it being an empty statue is 83.6%.

This is the obvious naive oversimplification. That's the average chance
but it can't be used to determine the overall odds of this happening; in
any given game the statues have a different chance of having a book. An
analysis based on the overall average chance neglects that book-full
statues tend to cluster (on deep Oracles) and so do book-empty statues (on
shallow Oracles); the odds that a given statue has a book are not
independent of the odds that its neighbours have books.

A DL 5 Oracle has an (11/12)^8 chance of no spellbook, about .499.
A DL 6-7 Oracle has an (11/13)^8 chance of no spellbook, about .263.
A DL 8-9 Oracle has an (11/14)^8 chance of no spellbook, about .145.

If Oracle levels are evenly distributed between the possible depths, that
gives a circa .263 chance of a bookless Oracle overall (it is coincidence
that that is the same as the DL 6-7 chance to 3 significant figures),
somewhat better than your .239. The chance of that in five games is then
.263^5, 1.26 x 10^3. This is near as dammit a 1 in 800 chance.

You expect to play 1/p games to see an event of probability p in one game.
You expect to play (1/p)S{n} + 1 games to see an event of probability p in
n+1 successive games, where S{n} was the expected number of games to see
the event in n successive games.

S{1} is 3.80.
S{2} is 15.5.
S{3} is 59.8.
S{4} is 228.
S{5} is 870 games.

That someone, playing Spork, has seen and noticed an event that you would
expect to see once if you played 870 successive games is not completely
remarkable, I would say.
